From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Tue, 10 May 1994 07:49:46 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-141] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

 nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu (nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-118:
>>From: R Bryner <R.Bryner@m.cc.utah.edu>
>>Could this not be abstracted to include any comodity, including money,
>>gold, corn, single crystals of Si, Drugs, Guns, MIP-years, or labor?  Each
>>pair of comodities would then have a relative intrest rate, with competative
>>markets in each.  For those of you who have not figured out what
>>denominating contracts in goods would do, think about taxation and
>>inflation:-)
>>
>As I understand it, all the interest rates would tend to be equal
>because lending would tend to flow from the lower interest combinations
>to the higher interest ones.

 There is nothing unusual about having different interest rates for
loans whose value is denominated in different goods. For instance, long
term US dollar interest rates are about 7.5%, whereas long term yen interest
rates are about 4%, implying that the dollar is likely to be debased
3.5% faster than the yen over the next 20-30 years. However, the claim
that each pair of commodities has its own rate is bogus. It is only the
good in which the future repayment occurrs that affects the rate.

>This reminds me of something I also wonder about....I like the
>idea of cyphercurrency, but I'm not sure how it can be backed
>with anything. (And if the governments fall, we won't be using
>their currencies.) Is there some way to connect the money to
>a physical object (like a gram of gold) without losing anonymity?
>How well can an economy work if there's a chance that anything
>you use to back the currency might suddenly become a great deal
>more common? (For instance, a gold-backed currency becomes
>relatively worthless if someone discovers a cheap way to refine
>gold from sea water.)

 I think most digicash proposals have a bank that would issue
and redeem the currency, and that could provide a means of tying
the digicash to something physical. However, such backing is not
essential. As long as the entity which issues that currency controls
the supply in such a way as to minimize fluctuations in prices, the
currency will be sound. As long as it is practical for people to switch
from poorly managed currencies to well managed ones, the issuers will
be motivated to keep their currencies sound. It has only been the
legal and accounting cost barriers to switching currencies that have
prevented this kind of competition from working in the past.

>with holes in them, trust in governments.....). Can anyone think of
>a necessity-backed currency?

 Salt was a currency around 2000 years ago.

 habs@panix.com ("Harry S. Hawk") writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-113:
>Of course taxes have been arround for a long time, not exactly
>evolutionary time but meme-wise, they are probally ESS -- why?

 Standard special interest group politics - each individual beneficiary
(including the government employees running the welfare programs) has more
incentive to promote the tax and spend system than any individual taxpayer
has to stop it, and the costs of adequately informing oneself about the
subject are more likely to exceed the benefits of that information for the
taxpayer than for someone whose primary source of income involves welfare.
For a thorough discussion of this analysis of interest group politics,
I recommend _The Rise and Decline of Nations_ by Mancur Olson, Yale
University Press, 1982.
