From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Tue, 10 May 1994 12:15:22 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-147] ECON: Cyphercurrency backing

At  2:38 AM 5/10/94 -0400, Alexander Chislenko wrote:
>  I see two major advantages of backing currency by a commodity:
>
>  - stability of the currency thru emission control (the amount of monetary
>    mass is/depends_on the amount of the underlying 'currency commodity'.

(What! My money is emitting?! 8^@)

I'm not too sure what Sasha meant by emission control. Amount of currency
in circulation? Value of currency?

Rather than stability I'd say the advantage is currency issuers can't
manipulate the value. And the amount is only subsidiarily relevant, since
it influences but does not determine the value of the commodity.

>  - stability thru the general agreement to use it, based on perceived
>    safety of always being able to exchange the currency back to the
>    underlying commodity (or, in case of the direct involvement of the
>    commodity in the transactions, return to its primary use).

Experience has shown backing is pretty irrelevant here. What people are
really counting on is that they can go to the grocery store tomorrow and
buy bread with it, something that demonstrably has nothing to do with the
backing or lack thereof. It's easier (maybe) to achieve this with backing,
but backing is neither necessary nor sufficient.

>  The problem with it is that the price of the underlying commodity
>immediately increases because it acquires a new artificially assigned value,
>and lots of resources start being wasted on production, transportation and
>safeguarding of this commodity, while it is being pushed from the original
>areas of its application by the prohibitively high costs.

Why is this a problem, artificial, or wasteful? All these labels imply that
there is a trivial alternative that keeps more resources out of money
production - even information resources - money for nothin'! Backed
currencies have certain properties, unbacked currencies have other
properties. Backed currencies may use more resources, but so do luxury
cars. Assuming people can choose freely between them, if they choose backed
currencies, then it's silly to call it wasteful. Just think of a way to
satisfy people using fewer resources & make a fortune. But don't ask me to
drive a model-T when I want a Cadillac.

>  Now, cyphercurrency allows perfect emission control,  which cannot be
>achieved with any commodity,

I'm still confused by this terminology. Also, did I miss something? Why is
it assumed cyphercurrency is unbacked?

>with tiny costs of emission, storage and cir-
>culation, little danger of theft or loss and no disruption for the economy
>(unless somebody is cheating with emission in which case [declaration of]
>backing is of no help anyway).

Maybe yes, maybe no. Declaring a backing and setting up a system to fulfill
it might serve as a mechanism for self-commitment and/or reputation. If the
amount invested in the backing system exceeds the return to cheating, the
commitment becomes credible. Of course, the money issuer can achieve this
without backing, by getting bonded or something.

>  Also, one should not expect that there will be just one cyphercurrency
>issued by one agency and backed by one commodity.
>Digitized stocks, bonds, personal promissory notes and other bit strings
>of any legal status and backing scheme may circulate freely in the market
>and exchange into each other at variable rates.

How about separating the unit of account from the medium of transactions?
What's the difference between issuing money and issuing credit? (This is
not intended as a rhetorical question implying they are the same -  rather,
how can they be made the same?) These and other related ideas are discussed
in:
   TITLE:  Explorations in the new monetary economics / Tyler Cowen and
                   Randall Kroszner.
   PUBLICATION:  Oxford, UK ; Cambridge, Mass., USA : Blackwell, 1994.
   DESCRIPTION:  vii, 200 p. ; 24 cm.


