Will this tend to keep cyphertransactions in relatively small amounts?
Counterfeiters love big transactions. The crypto guys seem to assume the
system has to be unbreakable or it won't work at all. At first glance, this
is quite plausible - the transactions costs of passing bad money would seem
much lower on the net. Given that reality, however, something could still
evolve where the payoff of counterfeiting was lower - small transactions or
some such. Or would that mean they'd have to be ridiculously, impractically
small? There's a non-zero risk to using any system of payments (cash =
theft, checks = forgery or bank failure, credit cards, etc.). Can
cyphercash lower the individual risk sufficiently? Does it involve more or
less systemic risk (risk that not just my money but everyone's will be
lost, that the system will crash)?

>Once desk-top manufacturing is common (and assuming that it implies
>easy extraction of minerals), perhaps only unique things will be
>valuable. This could have interesting implications for a currency,
